# The application: storage. Each role's initramfs provides its own binary under
# this one name, so PID 1 needs no configuration and there is nothing to
# mis-set.
#
# Started once the clock is synchronised (see `timesync`), and stopped if the
# keeper ends. When it ends — cleanly or not — the guest ends with it (see
# `boot`).
#
# It runs as a user of its own, `app` (1000, image/initramfs), handed what it
# opens — its volume among it — by `app-files`. As root it would own whatever
# root does, capabilities or not — the kernel's settings, PID 1's files — and
# `lockdown` keeping those read-only is then the second line, not the only one.
# It holds no capability: no `capabilities` raises any, nothing in the image
# carries file capabilities, and not being root, an exec gives it none, nor has
# it anything to become root again with. Storage needs none: it listens on
# vsock ports above the reserved ones.
#
# Its stdin, stdout and stderr are the console, as a process PID 1 starts has
# always had here: `console=null` on a production command line discards them,
# and the `debug` one puts a real console on the serial port. What may reach the
# host goes to the log device by name (crates/safe-logger), never through these.
type = process
command = /bin/app
depends-on: timesync
restart = false
run-as = app
options: shares-console
